the repercussions of a potentially  damaging trade war between the US and  China right now it remains a war of  words between the world's two largest  economies but yesterday stocks plunged  after the president threatened to  escalate the tariff battle and then  words by his Treasury Secretary added to  the fear the Dow finished down 572  points losing just over 2% of its value  Niki batiste is here with more Nicki  good morning good morning Friday capped  off a rollercoaster week for the markets  the ups and downs on Wall Street  reflected back and forth between the  United States and China as the two  countries continue to propose tariffs on  goods imported into each country this  sell-off started shortly after the start  of trading Friday morning as mounting  fears of a trade war with China weighed  on investors is this now a trade war mr.  secretary no it's not and our objective  is still not to be in a trade war with  them the Dow had already dropped more  than 300 points by 1:00 p.m. Eastern  when Treasury secretary Steve minuchin  appeared on CNBC on the one hand were  willing to continue negotiations on the  other hand the president is absolutely  prepared to defend our interests those  comments didn't seem to calm the markets  with the Dow dropping more than 600  points before ticking up slightly by  closing on Friday President Trump shook  off any concerns about short-term  fluctuations in a radio interview so we  may take a hit and you know what  ultimately we're going to be much  stronger for it but it's something we  had to do the president accuses China of  stealing US intellectual property and  other violations of global trade rules  last month in response the  administration announced tariffs on  imported Chinese aluminum and steel this  week China fired back threatening to put  tariffs on 50 billion dollars worth of  US agricultural products like pork and  soybeans now President Trump is said to  be considering 100 billion dollars of  additional tariffs in response nothing's  happened nothing's been executed White  House National Economic Council director  Larry Kudlow told reporters the u.s. is  open to negotiations  but said there should be no doubt who's  to blame blame China don't blame the  administration don't blame Japan don't  blame Europe blame China because they  have to put their best foot forward  and act like a world power the worry is  extending all the way to the Fed Federal  Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell says  businesses are expressing their concern  and that the prospect of a trade war  with China was discussed at the feds  March meeting Anthony Nicky batiste  thanks Nicky
 

